"""
TTL Cache with Logical Clock — hold a value, but only for a while.

WHAT IT DOES: Cached DB credentials, a session token, a DNS answer — things that
must expire so stale secrets don't linger. TTL = time-to-live: set an item, and after
ttl ticks it's gone. Uses a logical clock, not wall-clock: time only moves when YOU
move it (clock.advance(n)) — which makes tests deterministic and lets you simulate
hours in microseconds.

WHEN TO USE: You need a time-limited cache where expiry must be testable and
deterministic. The logical clock means your tests don't need time.sleep() — you
control time explicitly.

GUARANTEES: Items are returned when age ≤ ttl (strict: expired when age > ttl, not
age ≥ ttl). After expiry, get() returns None and deletes the item. Re-setting a key
resets its insertion time. The logical clock is deterministic — replaying the same
sequence produces identical results.

NOT: A production cache. size() returns the raw dictionary length, not the count of
non-expired items (docstring-lie). Expiry is LAZY — only checked on get(), so
write-heavy workloads with never-re-read keys leak memory unboundedly (fix #11).
The clock accepts advance(-n), which resurrects expired items.
"""
